user: I'm trying to stabilize the average ping in our game HUD, which has been around 95ms on mid-tier networks, and I want to make sure our LagCompensation module is working correctly. I've been looking at the SyncManager.js file, especially line 78, where we had an issue with inconsistent timestamp handling, but that's been fixed. Now, I'm focusing on the LagCompensation module with its interpolation and extrapolation algorithms to see if there's any room for improvement. Here's a snippet of what I have so far:
```javascript
class LagCompensation {
  constructor() {
    this.interpolationDelay = 100; // ms
    this.extrapolationLimit = 500; // ms
  }

  interpolate(playerState, serverState) {
    // Simple linear interpolation for now
    return playerState + (serverState - playerState) * 0.5;
  }

  extrapolate(playerState, deltaTime) {
    // Basic extrapolation based on last known velocity
    return playerState + deltaTime * 0.1;
  }
}

// Example usage in the game loop
const lagCompensation = new LagCompensation();
const playerState = { x: 10, y: 20 };
const serverState = { x: 15, y: 25 };
const interpolatedState = lagCompensation.interpolate(playerState, serverState);
console.log(interpolatedState);
```
I'm wondering if there's a way to further optimize this for our specific use case, considering we're using WebRTC for low-latency peer-to-peer communication and have upgraded to Matter.js v0.18.0 for improved collision resolution. Maybe there's a more sophisticated interpolation algorithm we could use?
